sion, using explicit facts, and state our current recommendation. We need to decide whether to stick with Candidate C or shift back to B. We must consider the evidence.\n\nWe have:\n\n- Candidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical competence), excellent computer skills (technical), but pretentious, grumpy, uncooperative, wrong tone, makes nasty remarks. No concrete examples of crew cooperation.\n\n- Candidate C: can anticipate dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership qualities (crew cooperation), but sometimes not good at taking criticism (cooperation), can be unorganized (attention/accuracy), sometimes not good at taking criticism (feedback orientation). No explicit reliability evidence.\n\n- Candidate A: quick correct decisions, positive atmosphere, 100% reliable, but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\n- Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, but arrogant, weak leadership, moody, loner.\n\nWe need to cover all candidates before final recommendation, but we can still argue. The current leading candidate per group is B, but Agent 1 currently voted C. We need to respond to Agent 3's statement, which supports B. We can either maintain C, argue why C still better, or shift to B. The instruction: \"Your message should be a short argumentative contribution, not a neutral evidence note.\" So we need to argue.\n\nWe should reference Agent 3's points: they note B's core safety attributes, but also note lack of concrete evidence of positive crew cooperation for B, and that C's unfriendly demeanor may be harder to remediate. We can counter that C's leadership qualities and ability to anticipate dangerous situations are critical for long-haul flights, and that the lack of explicit reliability evidence for C is a gap, but we have reliability for B (very conscientious). However, B's interpersonal issues are also serious. We could argue that the lack of concrete examples for B's crew cooperation is a bigger risk than C's unorganized habits, and that C's leadership may offset his criticism issues.\n\nWe need to decide vote. I think we can maintain vote for Candidate C, arguing that his ability to anticipate danger and leadership outweigh B's communication issues, especially given no concrete evidence of B's crew cooperation. Also note that C's criticism issue can be mitigated with training, while B's uncooperative behavior may be more ingrained.\n\nThus we will keep vote for Candidate C. Provide reasoning: C's anticipation of dangerous situations is a unique safety advantage, leadership fosters crew cooperation, and his technical vision supports long-haul tasks. B's interpersonal deficits are documented and lack evidence of mitigation. Also note that reliability for C is not documented, but we have no evidence of unreliability; we cannot assume lack. Meanwhile, B's pretentiousness and uncooperative behavior may be more detrimental.\n\nThus we will produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: \"I remain convinced that Candidate C is the stronger overall fit because...\" referencing facts. Also refer to Agent 3's points.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate C\"."}
